 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 21,
   "y": 19,
   "plaque": "brighton_dean_mahomed",
   "title": "Sake Dean Mahomed",
   "text": "Sake Dean Mahomed, from Patna in India, was the first Indian to publish a book in English, in 1794, and opened London's first Indian restaurant, the Hindoostane Coffee House, in 1810. In Brighton he ran a seafront bath house offering shampooing, an Indian massage, and became Shampooing Surgeon to George IV. Every bottle of shampoo owes him a nod.",
   "people": "south_asian"
  },
  {
   "x": 37,
   "y": 2,
   "text": "BRIGHTON. London 54 miles behind you, the sea about half a mile in front. Please drive slowly: the gulls are crossing."
  },
  {
   "x": 14,
   "y": 4,
   "text": "The London and Brighton Railway reached here in 1841, and a fishing town with a royal palace became London's seaside. Day trippers could now come down and go home the same day, and they still do, by the hundred thousand.",
   "plaque": "brighton_station",
   "title": "Brighton Station"
  },
  {
   "x": 20,
   "y": 17,
   "text": "The Prince Regent, later George IV, came to Brighton for sea bathing and fun, and had John Nash turn his seaside villa into this between 1815 and 1823: Indian domes and minarets outside, Chinese dragons and bamboo inside. Queen Victoria found it too public, sold it to the town in 1850, and took the furniture.",
   "plaque": "brighton_royal_pavilion",
   "title": "The Royal Pavilion"
  },
  {
   "x": 23,
   "y": 18,
   "text": "In the First World War the Pavilion and the Dome became a hospital for soldiers of the Indian Army wounded on the Western Front. Over 2,000 were treated here. The Chattri, a memorial on the downs above Brighton, marks where Hindu and Sikh soldiers who died were cremated.",
   "plaque": "brighton_pavilion_hospital",
   "people": "south_asian",
   "title": "A Hospital in the Palace"
  },
  {
   "x": 37,
   "y": 8,
   "text": "The laines were the old open fields round the fishing town, divided into long strips. When the town grew, streets of little terraced houses were built along the strips, and the North Laine is now a maze of independent shops, cafés and buskers. Somebody here will sell you a vintage parka.",
   "plaque": "brighton_north_laine",
   "title": "The North Laine"
  },
  {
   "x": 2,
   "y": 26,
   "text": "The oldest part of Brighton, when it was the fishing village of Brighthelmstone: narrow alleys, called twittens in Sussex, between flint-faced cottages. Now they are full of jewellers and antique shops, and it is still very easy to get lost in them.",
   "plaque": "brighton_lanes",
   "title": "The Lanes"
  },
  {
   "x": 18,
   "y": 32,
   "text": "Many old Brighton walls are built of cobbles: flints rounded by the sea and picked off the beach, set in lime mortar with brick corners to hold the edges straight. The beach was the builders' merchant, and the sea delivered for free.",
   "plaque": "brighton_flint",
   "title": "Flint Cobbles"
  },
  {
   "x": 45,
   "y": 28,
   "text": "Brighton has been a haven for LGBTQ+ people for a very long time, and its Pride, which began with a small march in 1973 and grew into a festival every August, is one of the biggest in the UK. The whole town turns rainbow for a weekend, including several of the gulls.",
   "plaque": "brighton_pride",
   "title": "Brighton Pride"
  },
  {
   "x": 41,
   "y": 25,
   "text": "The fountain in the Old Steine was put up in 1846, with three dolphins holding up its bowls. The Steine itself was a flat open space where fishermen dried their nets, before it was the smartest place in town to be seen.",
   "plaque": "brighton_victoria_fountain",
   "title": "The Victoria Fountain"
  },
  {
   "x": 37,
   "y": 35,
   "text": "Opened in 1872, this is the oldest working aquarium in the world. Its long underground hall was built beneath the road by the pier, with columns of granite and marble and a ceiling of vaulted brick. The fish have not complained about the décor.",
   "plaque": "brighton_aquarium",
   "title": "Brighton Aquarium"
  },
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 42,
   "text": "Opened in 1899, over half a kilometre long, with its arcades, its helter skelter and its fish and chips on the end. On winter evenings the starlings gather under the deck in their thousands before they settle to roost among the ironwork.",
   "plaque": "brighton_palace_pier",
   "title": "The Palace Pier"
  },
  {
   "x": 9,
   "y": 43,
   "text": "Built in 1866 by Eugenius Birch, the West Pier was a favourite of the Victorians. It closed in 1975, was battered by storms and burned twice in 2003. Its iron skeleton still stands out in the sea, and on winter evenings starlings swirl around it in great murmurations.",
   "plaque": "brighton_west_pier",
   "title": "The West Pier"
  },
  {
   "x": 4,
   "y": 43,
   "text": "A slender observation tower 162 metres tall, opened in 2016 by the people who made the London Eye. A doughnut-shaped glass pod slides up the mast and gives you the coast from Beachy Head to the Isle of Wight on a clear day.",
   "plaque": "brighton_i360",
   "title": "The i360"
  },
  {
   "x": 52,
   "y": 43,
   "text": "Magnus Volk opened his little railway along the beach in 1883. It is the oldest electric railway in the world still running. For a few years he also ran the 'Daddy Long Legs', a tramcar on stilts that waded through the sea on rails laid on the seabed.",
   "plaque": "brighton_volks",
   "title": "Volk's Electric Railway"
  },
  {
   "x": 60,
   "y": 37,
   "text": "Over the Whitsun bank holiday of 1964, mods on Italian scooters and rockers on motorbikes clashed on Brighton's seafront, as they did at Margate and Clacton. The papers went wild. The film Quadrophenia (1979) is set here, and scooter rallies still roar down Madeira Drive.",
   "plaque": "brighton_mods_rockers",
   "title": "Mods and Rockers, 1964"
  },
  {
   "x": 24,
   "y": 43,
   "text": "No sand here: Brighton's beach is shingle, flint pebbles rounded by the waves and rolled along the coast. Wooden groynes stop it drifting east. Bring shoes, a cushion, or a lot of determination.",
   "plaque": "brighton_shingle",
   "title": "The Pebble Beach"
  }
 ],
